<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José Figueres Ferrer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1906">1906</a>–<a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José Figueres Ferrer was a Costa Rican politician who led the victorious side in the civil war of 1948 and then headed a governing junta for eighteen months. His junta abolished the army, nationalised the banks and extended full voting rights to women and Afro-Costa Ricans. He founded the National Liberation Party and was elected president twice, in 1953–1958 and 1970–1974.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-figueres-ferrer.jpg" alt="José Figueres Ferrer" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MadriCR · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jos%C3%A9_Figueres_Ferrer_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José Figueres Ferrer was born on 25 September 1906 in San Ramón, the eldest of four children of a doctor and a teacher who had recently come from Catalonia, and his first language was Catalan. In 1924 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> and studied hydroelectric engineering at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a> in 1928 he bought a remote mountain farm which he named La Lucha Sin Fin, the Endless Struggle, and which remained his home until his death. He became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> grower and rope manufacturer employing more than 1,000 sharecroppers and factory workers, and called himself a farmer-socialist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Figueres returned from two years of exile in 1944, having criticised President Rafael Ángel Calderón Guardia, and founded a Democratic Action current that became the Social Democratic Party. The 1 March 1948 presidential election, in which Otilio Ulate had apparently defeated Calderón, was annulled by Calderón's supporters. Protests grew into armed conflict, and Figueres defeated the Costa Rican Army and communist-led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> who had joined President Teodoro Picado. The 44-day civil war killed more than 2,000 people and was the bloodiest event of twentieth-century Costa Rican history.</p><p>Figueres then headed a provisional junta, the Founding Council, for eighteen months. It abolished the army, nationalised the banks, enabled women and illiterates to vote, gave citizenship to the children of Black immigrants, outlawed the Communist Party, set up a civil service and directed the writing of a new constitution. Figueres later said it had decreed 834 reforms. He then handed power to Ulate. In 1953 he created the National Liberation Party and was elected president.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>During his presidency from 1953 to 1958 Figueres promoted private industry and the national industrial sector and helped build the Pan-American Highway. Implicated in an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> by exiles in April 1954, he faced an invasion led by Calderón in January 1955 and promised the Nicaraguan ruler to sever links with the exiles. He survived an assassination attempt in 1957. Elected again, he served from 1970 to 1974; in 1972 he sold 30,000 tons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a> established diplomatic relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><p>His administrations created the Costa Rica Institute of Technology, the National University and the Costa Rican Electricity Institute as an autonomous body. He died on 8 June 1990 in San José at the age of 83 and was given a state funeral. His son José María Figueres was president from 1994 to 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “José Figueres Ferrer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 1 December 1948 President <b>José Figueres Ferrer</b> of Costa Rica abolished the Costa Rican military, and in 1949 the abolition was introduced in Article 12 of the constitution.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pacifism">Pacifism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After a short civil war in 1948, <b>José Figueres</b> abolished the army, and the 1949 constitution confirmed it.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-costa-rica">Republic of Costa Rica</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in San Ramón, in what is now <a href="/countries/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-jose-costa-rica">San José</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=10.09,-84.47&amp;year=1960" aria-label="San Ramón on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of José Figueres Ferrer"><image href="/maps/globes/g27000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5465 4142h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5465 4142h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=10.09,-84.47&amp;year=1960">What was here in 1960? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
